\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 And what about the packet data?
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 You have a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 second allocation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 :
\par m->m_datastart = malloc(len);
\par That should ideally get its own abstraction too:
\par void *mbuf_data_alloc(size_t len)
\par \{
\par     return malloc(len);
\par \}
\par 
\par void mbuf_data_free(void *p)
\par \{
\par     free(p);
\par \}
\par Then m_freem() becomes:
\par void m_freem(struct mbuf *m)
\par \{
\par     if (!m)
\par         return;
\par 
\par     while (m)
\par     \{
\par         struct mbuf *next = m->m_next;
\par 
\par         if (m->m_datastart)
\par             mbuf_data_free(m->m_datastart);
\par 
\par         mbuf_free(m);
\par 
\par         m = next;
\par     \}
\par \}
\par And mbuf_from_buffer():
\par m->m_datastart = mbuf_data_alloc(len);
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 Therefore }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid8871807 ZEURE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486  abstraction boundary is:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486              }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid16015552 DRIVER}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486  code
\par                  |
\par                  | m_freem(m)
\par                  v
\par         +------------------+
\par         |    m_freem()     |  <-- understands mbuf chains
\par         +------------------+
\par              |        |
\par              |        |
\par              v        v
\par       mbuf_data_free  mbuf_free
\par              |        |
\par              v        v
\par           free()    free()
\par And allocation:
\par              proto_main_loop()
\par                     |
\par                     v
\par                mbuf_alloc()
\par                     |
\par                     v
\par                   malloc()
\par Later, you can replace the bottom layer with pools without touching the main loop or the dozens of m_freem() calls.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 So, to answer }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid8871807 ZEURE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486  question directly
\par Yes: replace the free(m) inside m_freem() with mbuf_free(m).}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 No: don't replace all }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid8871807 ZEURE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 
 existing m_freem(m) calls with mbuf_free(m).}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 
\par m_freem() is the existing higher-level API that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8871807 ZEURE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486  code should continue to use.
\par mbuf_alloc() / mbuf_free() are the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 allocation/deallocation abstraction layer underneath it}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 .
\par That is probably the cleanest and least disruptive way to satisfy }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid8871807 ZEURE}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486 
 colleague's "no malloc in the main loop" requirement while keeping the existing }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid16015552 DRIVER}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid9919486\charrsid9919486  behaviour intact.
